  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 148 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nFriday/Friday night\nAssessment...low (<20%) confidence on rain\n\nProgged soundings of the atmosphere continue to show dry air in the \nlow levels through Friday morning. Thus rain is questionable but \nwith moisture in the mid-levels, sprinkles are on the table.\n\nLike late Thursday night the rain chances generally look to be south \nof an Ottumwa, IA to Galesburg, IL line. Thus will keep 20-40% \nchances going for that area with slightly higher chances south of \nthe IA/MO border.\n\nFriday night the models show rain chances on the warm advection \nassociated with the expanding heat dome. Again, the rain chances are \nnot high; 20-40 percent south of an Ottumwa, IA to Galesburg, IL \nline with most of the area remaining dry.\n\nSaturday through Sunday\nAssessment...very high (>95%) confidence of heat and humidity\nreturning\n\nThe southerly flow that develops Friday night will continue through \nthe weekend allowing heat and humidity to return. While the humidity \nwill be noticeable Saturday, Sunday will be downright uncomfortable \nwith dew points generally 70 to 75. Heat indices on Sunday look to \napproach 100 and may exceed 100 in some areas.\n\nSunday night through Tuesday\nAssessment...high (>80%) confidence on heat and humidity continuing\n\nThe heat and humidity will continue into the first part of the work \nweek. Heat indices may again approach or exceed 100 on Monday which \nraising the question of potential heat headlines Sunday through \nMonday evening. \n\nThe wild card is where the ring of fire sets up. Organized storm \ncomplexes should move across Minnesota in Wisconsin before turning \nsouth and potentially moving into northern Illinois. The various \nmodels suggest the far northeast and eastern area could get side-\nswiped by a storm complex late Sunday night into Monday morning and \nagain late Monday night into Tuesday. If this scenario does occur, \nit could affect any heat headlines for Monday.\n\nThe key will be where do the storm complexes Saturday night into \nSunday develop and go. This will impact where the storm complex \nSunday night into Monday forms and goes and likewise the Monday \nnight into Tuesday complex.\n\nTuesday night/Wednesday\nAssessment...a certainty (>95%) of continued warm and humid\nconditions\n\nBy Tuesday night the tropical connection with the Gulf weakens which \nwill allow dew points to generally drop back into the 65 to 70 \nrange; humid but not oppressive. The heat dome ridge partially de-\namplifies and turns the mid-level flow more west to east across the \nMidwest. As a result, the organized storm complexes should remain \nacross Minnesota and Wisconsin.",
